    "notes": "Owner-FCF series (reported FCF - SBC - equity-award withholding taxes): FY2020 2,538; FY2022 1,746; FY2025 3,891 ($M). The 3-year FCF CAGR (30.6%) runs from a FY2022 working-capital/supply-chain trough and is not a forward rate; the 5-year (8.9%) is the honest one. Owner and reported 5-year FCF CAGRs match, so SBC is not outgrowing cash flow. Revenue, EPS and equity CAGRs all include acquisitions (Wright Medical FY2020, Vocera FY2023, Inari FY2025) -- the 11.4% selected equity CAGR overstates organic capacity, so projected growth starts from the FY2026 ORGANIC guidance (8.3-9.3%) instead. EPS 5-year CAGR flattered by a COVID-depressed FY2020 base (4.26)."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Stryker lands FY2026 near, but slightly under, its 8.3-9.3% organic guide: Q2's +9% organic shows the March cyberattack was a timing disruption, but H1 reported growth of only 6.1% leaves a demanding H2. From there the two engines -- Mako robotic placements pulling through knee/hip/shoulder implants, and MedSurg & Neurotechnology (Inari venous thromboembolism, neurovascular, endoscopy, medical beds/stretchers) -- keep organic growth high-single-digit for several years before maturing toward 4%. Mix shift toward the lower-gross-margin MedSurg segment caps the margin gain at ~50-60bp/yr, ending at ~30.5% pre-SBC GAAP EBITDA. No buybacks are assumed to create value (the flat count is held by expensing SBC); M&A is excluded, so growth is organic only.",
    "bull": "Mako's ~2,700-system installed base widens its lead over Zimmer's ROSA and J&J's VELYS as spine and shoulder applications launch, keeping Orthopaedics growing mid-to-high single digit and lifting implant mix; Inari is cross-sold through Stryker's hospital relationships and the VTE market grows double-digit. Low China exposure (~2%) shields Stryker from VBP and tariff escalation relative to peers. Organic growth holds ~9% through 2030 and operating leverage plus manufacturing productivity take pre-SBC EBITDA margin to 32.5%. Market re-rates toward its historic premium multiple.",
    "bear": "The cyberattack proves more than a timing issue -- lost capital-equipment orders and hospital trust push FY2026 below guide -- while ROSA and VELYS close the robotics gap and hospital capex budgets tighten. MedSurg (lower margin) keeps outgrowing Orthopaedics, tariffs (~$175M in FY2025, unquantified for 2026) recur, and legacy hip litigation (MDL 2768/2441) and post-Inari leverage absorb cash. Growth decays to ~3% and margins stall at ~27.5%; the market pays a mid-teens FCF multiple for a slower, ex-premium medtech."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Unlevered owner-FCF DCF with SBC plus equity-award withholding taxes fully expensed and the diluted count held flat. Growth is organic only; acquisitions are neither modelled nor charged. GAAP special items are treated as recurring costs. Acquired-intangible amortization is run off so the terminal tax shield is not perpetuated.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Terminal value is the majority of EV; a 1pp WACC change moves value roughly 15-20%.",
      "M&A is excluded: Stryker's history of buying growth means reported revenue will likely outrun these organic paths, but at a cash cost this model does not charge.",
      "FY2026 H2 must deliver ~11% growth to meet guidance after a cyberattack-hit Q1.",
      "Robotics competition (ROSA, VELYS) and MedSurg mix shift both cap margin expansion.",
      "Legacy hip-implant litigation and tariffs are unquantified cash risks."
    ]
  },
